= Solution
{parent=a}

The double-angle <trigonometric identity> $\sin2x=2\sin x\cos x$ makes the integrand $2\sin x\cos^2x$. The <integration by substitution> $u=\cos x$, $du=-\sin x\,dx$, gives
$$
\int\sin2x\cos x\,dx=-2\int u^2\,du=\boxed{-\frac23\cos^3x+C}.
$$

= b
{parent=2}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use <integration by parts> with $u=\ln x$ and $dv=x\,dx$. Since $du=dx/x$ and $v=x^2/2$,
$$
\int x\ln x\,dx=\frac{x^2}2\ln x-\frac12\int x\,dx
=\boxed{\frac{x^2}2\ln x-\frac{x^2}4+C},\qquad x>0.
$$

