Rewrite the <exponential function> as $2^x=e^{x\ln2}$. The <chain rule> gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{dy}{dx}=2^x\ln2.}
$$

= b
{parent=3}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Differentiate each coordinate with respect to the parameter $t$ and use the <chain rule>:
$$
\frac{dy}{dx}=\frac{dy/dt}{dx/dt}
=\frac{\sec^2t}{-\csc^2t}=\boxed{-\tan^2t}.
$$
Both coordinates are defined where $\sin t\ne0$ and $\cos t\ne0$. As a check, $xy=1$ there, so the same <derivative> is $-1/x^2$.

