Writing the <polynomial> as $p(x)$, direct evaluation gives $p(0)=-6$ and $p(3)=42$. A <polynomial> is continuous, so the <intermediate value theorem> guarantees a <polynomial root> between them. Trying the integer $2$ gives $16+12-22-6=0$, hence **$x=2$ is a root**.

= b
{parent=5}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

By the <factor theorem>, $x-2$ divides the <polynomial>. Division gives $2x^2+7x+3=(2x+1)(x+3)$, so
$$
\boxed{2x^3+3x^2-11x-6=(x-2)(2x+1)(x+3).}
$$
Its three <polynomial roots> are $2,-1/2,-3$.

