The <binomial theorem> gives coefficients $1,4,6,4,1$ for the fourth power. Substituting $2x$ for its variable gives
$$
\boxed{1+8x+24x^2+32x^3+16x^4=(1+2x)^4,\qquad a=1,\ b=2,\ m=4.}
$$

= b
{parent=6}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <function graph> is a horizontally shifted and scaled fourth power. It is nonnegative and touches the horizontal axis at its fourfold <polynomial root>. The axis intersections are
$$
\boxed{(-1/2,0)\quad\text{and}\quad(0,1).}
$$
The <derivative> is $8(1+2x)^3$, so the graph decreases to its flat <local minimum> at $x=-1/2$ and then increases. Its endpoint values on the requested interval are $f(-1)=1$ and $f(1)=81$. The original sketch includes a closer view of the minimum so that the small values are visible beside the much larger right endpoint.

