Put $\theta=\pi/12$. The double-angle <trigonometric identities> imply $\cos^2(2\theta)=1-\sin^2(2\theta)$. Since $2\theta=\pi/6$ is in the first quadrant, the positive <square root> is required. The <half-angle identities> then give the requested expression in terms of $\sin(\pi/6)$:
$$
\boxed{\sin^2\frac\pi{12}=\frac{1-\sqrt{1-\sin^2(\pi/6)}}2.}
$$

= b
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Using $\sin(\pi/6)=1/2$ in the preceding <half-angle identities> gives
$$
\sin^2\frac\pi{12}=\frac{2-\sqrt3}{4}\approx\frac{2-1.732}{4}=0.067.
$$
To two decimal places the answer is **$0.07$**.

