= Solution
{parent=a}

In <polar coordinates>, $x=r\cos\theta$, $y=r\sin\theta$, with $r=\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$. Thus the <polar form of a complex number> is
$$
\boxed{z=r(\cos\theta+i\sin\theta)=re^{i\theta}.}
$$
Here $r=|z|\geq0$ is its <complex modulus>; for $z\ne0$, the <complex argument> $\theta$ is determined modulo $2\pi$. At $z=0$ the angle is undefined, although every value of $\theta$ gives the same point when $r=0$.

= b
{parent=11}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For $z\ne0$, the <complex logarithm> is $\ln z=\ln r+i(\theta+2\pi k)$, where $k\in\mathbb Z$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Re}\ln z=\frac12\ln(x^2+y^2),\qquad
\operatorname{Im}\ln z=\arg(x+iy)+2\pi k.}
$$
A specified branch chooses one continuous determination of the <complex argument> on an appropriate cut domain. For the usual principal-value convention use the <principal argument> $\operatorname{Arg}(x+iy)\in(-\pi,\pi]$. A formula using only $\arctan(y/x)$ would lose the quadrant information; it is a valid local angle only with the appropriate additive multiple of $\pi$.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Multiply numerator and denominator by the <complex conjugate>:
$$
\frac1z=\frac{x-iy}{x^2+y^2}.
$$
Therefore, away from the origin,
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Re}(1/z)=\frac{x}{x^2+y^2},\qquad
\operatorname{Im}(1/z)=-\frac{y}{x^2+y^2}.}
$$

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

Use $|z|^2=z\bar z$ and $z-\bar z=2iy$. Then
$$
\bar z(z^2-|z|^2)=\bar z\,z(z-\bar z)=2iy|z|^2=2iy(x^2+y^2).
$$
The <real part> and <imaginary part> are therefore
$$
\boxed{0\quad\text{and}\quad2y(x^2+y^2),\ \text{respectively}.}
$$

= iv
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iv}

Expand the defining <complex exponential functions>:
$$
\cosh(x+iy)=\frac{e^{x+iy}+e^{-x-iy}}2
=\cosh x\cos y+i\sinh x\sin y.
$$
Thus the <real part> and <imaginary part> of the <hyperbolic cosine> are
$$
\boxed{\operatorname{Re}\cosh z=\cosh x\cos y,\qquad
\operatorname{Im}\cosh z=\sinh x\sin y.}
$$

= c
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

A constant <real part> $\operatorname{Re}\ln z=L$ means $\ln|z|=L$, so its <level sets> are the concentric <circles>
$$
\boxed{x^2+y^2=e^{2L},\qquad L\in\mathbb R.}
$$
Their radii are strictly positive. The <real part> is independent of the logarithm's branch, so there is no branch ambiguity in these <circles>.

For $\operatorname{Re}(1/z)=K$, the equation is $x=K(x^2+y^2)$. If $K\ne0$, complete the square to obtain
$$
\boxed{\left(x-\frac1{2K}\right)^2+y^2=\frac1{4K^2},\qquad (x,y)\ne(0,0).}
$$
These <circles> have centres on the real axis and are tangent to the imaginary axis at the excluded origin. Positive $K$ puts the <circle> to the right, negative $K$ to the left. The separate case $K=0$ gives the punctured imaginary axis $\boxed{x=0,\ y\ne0}$. The sketch uses an open marker at the origin to emphasize that $1/z$ is never defined there.

\Image[paper-1-complex-contours.png]
{title=Constant real-part contours of the complex logarithm and reciprocal}

= d
{parent=11}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Set $w=e^z\ne0$. The <hyperbolic cosine> equation becomes $w+w^{-1}=-4$, hence $w^2+4w+1=0$. Its two <polynomial roots> are $w=-2\pm\sqrt3$, both negative. Taking every value of the <complex logarithm> yields
$$
z=\ln|w|+i(2k+1)\pi,\qquad k\in\mathbb Z.
$$
Since $(2-\sqrt3)(2+\sqrt3)=1$, their real logarithms have opposite signs. All solutions are therefore
$$
\boxed{z=\pm\ln(2+\sqrt3)+i(2k+1)\pi,\qquad k\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
The positive real constant is also $\operatorname{arcosh}2$, the <inverse hyperbolic cosine> of $2$. Substitution gives $\cosh z=-\cosh(\ln(2+\sqrt3))=-2$ for both signs and every integer $k$.

= 12
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=12}
{scope}

= i
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Assume $F$ is <continuously differentiable> near the point of interest on $F=0$, and that the relevant denominators below are nonzero. The <total differential> on the surface is
$$
dF=F_x\,dx+F_y\,dy+F_z\,dz=0.
$$
Holding $z$ fixed gives $F_x\,dx+F_y\,dy=0$, so the <implicit function theorem> gives
$$
\left(\frac{\partial y}{\partial x}\right)_z=-\frac{F_x}{F_y},\qquad
\left(\frac{\partial x}{\partial y}\right)_z=-\frac{F_y}{F_x}.
$$
When $F_xF_y\ne0$, their product proves the <reciprocity rule for constrained partial derivatives>:
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac{\partial y}{\partial x}\right)_z
\left(\frac{\partial x}{\partial y}\right)_z=1.}
$$
Similarly, holding $y$ or $x$ fixed gives $(\partial x/\partial z)_y=-F_z/F_x$ and $(\partial z/\partial y)_x=-F_y/F_z$. With $F_xF_yF_z\ne0$, multiply the three ratios:
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac{\partial y}{\partial x}\right)_z
\left(\frac{\partial x}{\partial z}\right)_y
\left(\frac{\partial z}{\partial y}\right)_x
=\left(-\frac{F_x}{F_y}\right)\left(-\frac{F_z}{F_x}\right)\left(-\frac{F_y}{F_z}\right)=-1.}
$$
This is the <cyclic partial derivative identity>. Nonzero denominators and local differentiable coordinate representations are substantive assumptions: at a singular coordinate representation, a formal cancellation of zero or infinite factors is not a valid proof.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Differentiate the defining function with the other coordinates independent. Put $C=\cosh(x+z)$ for brevity. Then
$$
F_x=yz-C,\qquad F_y=xz,\qquad F_z=xy-C.
$$
The <implicit differentiation> ratios therefore give
$$
\boxed{\left(\frac{\partial x}{\partial y}\right)_z=-\frac{xz}{yz-C},\quad
\left(\frac{\partial x}{\partial z}\right)_y=-\frac{xy-C}{yz-C},\quad
\left(\frac{\partial y}{\partial z}\right)_x=-\frac{xy-C}{xz}.}
$$
Each expression applies on the implicit surface wherever its own denominator is nonzero. The <hyperbolic cosine> appears because the <derivative> of $\sinh(x+z)$ with respect to either $x$ or $z$ is $\cosh(x+z)$.

= b
{parent=12}
{scope}

= i
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

There is a global-domain issue in the printed question. Write $D=a^2x^2+b^2y^2>0$ away from the origin. Direct <partial differentiation> gives
$$
P_y=-\frac{ab(a^2x^2-b^2y^2)}{D^2}
=Q_x.
$$
Thus the <differential one-form> is a <closed differential form> for **every real $a,b\ne0$**. The <Poincaré lemma> makes it locally an <exact differential form> on small neighborhoods avoiding the origin. There is no additional condition such as $a^2=b^2$.

However, the stated domain is the whole <punctured plane>, which is not <simply connected>. To test global exactness, use the closed <ellipse> $x=\cos t/a$, $y=\sin t/b$, $0\leq t\leq2\pi$. Here $D=1$ and
$$
P\,dx+Q\,dy=ab(x\,dy-y\,dx)=dt,\qquad
\oint(P\,dx+Q\,dy)=2\pi.
$$
The <line integral> of a globally <exact differential form> around any closed curve would be zero. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\text{globally exact on }\mathbb R^2\setminus\{0\}:\ \text{no allowed }a,b;\qquad
\text{locally exact}:\ \text{all }a,b\ne0.}
$$
The distinction is precisely that of the <elliptically scaled angular one-form>. The remaining parts can be answered consistently using a local angle branch, as follows.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

On a chart with $x\ne0$, set
$$
\boxed{f(x,y)=\arctan\!\left(\frac{by}{ax}\right)+C.}
$$
The <chain rule> verifies both components:
$$
f_x=\frac1{1+(by/(ax))^2}\left(-\frac{by}{ax^2}\right)
=-\frac{aby}{D}=P,\qquad
f_y=\frac1{1+(by/(ax))^2}\frac{b}{ax}
=\frac{abx}{D}=Q.
$$
A coordinate-independent description of the local potential is a continuous branch of $\arg(ax+iby)$, the <complex argument> of the scaled coordinates. Such branches can also cover points where $x=0$, using a different angular chart. Continuing once around the missing origin changes that angle by a nonzero multiple of $2\pi$, so **there is no global single-valued $f$ on the full stated domain**.

= iii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The nonzero factor $ab/D$ can be cancelled in the local <differential equation>, leaving $x\,dy-y\,dx=0$. On an interval with $x\ne0$,
$$
\frac{d}{dx}\left(\frac yx\right)=\frac{xy'-y}{x^2}=0.
$$
Hence the explicit <general solution> is
$$
\boxed{y(x)=Cx,\qquad C\in\mathbb R,}
$$
on any interval that avoids the excluded origin. It also follows from fixing the local angle potential, with slope $C=(a/b)\tan(f-C_0)$. As parameterized curves there are also vertical rays $x=0$, which cannot be represented as single-valued functions $y(x)$. Geometrically the connected <integral curves> are rays, not lines continued through the undefined origin.

