= Solution
{parent=a}

With the first $n$ terms counted from the constant term, <Taylor's theorem> gives
$$
\boxed{f(x)=\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\frac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!}(x-a)^k+R_n(x),\qquad
R_n(x)=\frac{f^{(n)}(\xi)}{n!}(x-a)^n,}
$$
where $\xi$ lies between $a$ and $x$. Continuous <derivatives> through order $n$ on the joining interval are a sufficient hypothesis for this <Taylor theorem with Lagrange remainder>. The <Taylor remainder> measures the error of the finite <Taylor polynomial>; convergence of the corresponding infinite <Taylor series> is a separate question.

= b
{parent=15}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For $f(x)=x^{1/3}$, the required <derivatives> are
$$
f'(x)=\frac13x^{-2/3},\quad f''(x)=-\frac29x^{-5/3},\quad
f^{(3)}(x)=\frac{10}{27}x^{-8/3}.
$$
At $a=8$ these give $f(8)=2$, $f'(8)=1/12$, and $f''(8)=-1/144$. Setting $x-a=1$, the three-term <Taylor polynomial> yields
$$
\boxed{9^{1/3}\approx2+\frac1{12}-\frac1{288}=\frac{599}{288}\approx2.07986111.}
$$
By the <Taylor theorem with Lagrange remainder>, the signed error is
$$
R_3=\frac{f^{(3)}(\xi)}{6}=\frac5{81}\xi^{-8/3},\qquad 8<\xi<9.
$$
It is positive, and $\xi^{-8/3}<8^{-8/3}=1/256$. Consequently
$$
\boxed{0<9^{1/3}-\frac{599}{288}<\frac5{20736}<\frac1{4000}.}
$$
The last comparison is exact because $5\cdot4000=20000<20736$. Thus the estimate is an underestimate with the required rigorous <absolute error> bound.

= c
{parent=15}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

Use the <Maclaurin series> $\sin x=x-x^3/6+O(x^5)$ and $e^u=1+u+u^2/2+u^3/6+u^4/24+O(u^5)$. The terms through fourth degree give
$$
\begin{aligned}
e^{\sin x}
&=1+\left(x-\frac{x^3}{6}\right)
+\frac12\left(x^2-\frac{x^4}{3}\right)
+\frac{x^3}{6}+\frac{x^4}{24}+O(x^5)\\
&=\boxed{1+x+\frac{x^2}{2}-\frac{x^4}{8}+O(x^5).}
\end{aligned}
$$
In particular the cubic coefficient cancels; it has not been omitted accidentally.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The <Maclaurin series> give $e^{-x^3}=1-x^3+O(x^6)$ and $\cosh x=1+x^2/2+x^4/24+O(x^6)$. Inverting the latter using the <geometric series> gives
$$
\frac1{\cosh x}=1-\frac{x^2}{2}+\left(\frac14-\frac1{24}\right)x^4+O(x^6)
=1-\frac{x^2}{2}+\frac5{24}x^4+O(x^6).
$$
Multiplying the two expansions yields
$$
\boxed{\frac{e^{-x^3}}{\cosh x}=1-\frac{x^2}{2}-x^3+\frac5{24}x^4+O(x^5).}
$$
The remainder is $O(x^5)$ because the product already generates an $x^5$ term from $(-x^3)(-x^2/2)$.

= 16
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

A uniformly shuffled deck makes all unordered five-card hands equally likely, giving $\binom{52}{5}$ possible hands. Choose the rank appearing four times in $13$ ways; all four suits of that rank are then fixed. The remaining card can be any of the other $48$ cards. No hand is counted twice, because five cards cannot contain two different complete four-card ranks. This use of <combinatorics> gives the <probability>
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(\text{four of a kind})=\frac{13\cdot48}{\binom{52}{5}}
=\frac{624}{\binom{52}{5}},\qquad N=624.}
$$
Here $\binom{52}{5}=2\,598\,960$ is a <binomial coefficient>; the <probability> also simplifies to $1/4165\approx0.000240096$.

= b
{parent=16}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Assume that wrong-note events are <independent events> and that each note has the same error <probability> $p$, with $0\leq p\leq1$. Then their count $N$ has a <binomial distribution>. Choose the $n$ mistaken positions in $\binom{100}{n}$ ways and multiply the probabilities of the errors and correct notes:
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(N=n)=\binom{100}{n}p^n(1-p)^{100-n},\qquad n=0,1,\ldots,100.}
$$
The endpoint cases $p=0$ or $p=1$ are understood by continuity, giving deterministic counts. More than half means at least $51$ mistakes, so
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(\text{booing})=\sum_{n=51}^{100}\binom{100}{n}p^n(1-p)^{100-n}.}
$$
The single-note error <probability> alone would not determine this count distribution if mistakes were correlated; <independence> is the additional standard modeling assumption.

= c
{parent=16}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

A <probability density function> must be nonnegative. The printed condition $s\ne0$ is therefore too weak: for $s<0$ the given prefactor makes the function negative everywhere. The <location-scale form of the logistic distribution> requires **$s>0$**, which is assumed in the remaining parts.

Set $t=(x-\mu)/(2s)$, so $dx=2s\,dt$. The <normalization of a probability distribution> follows from the <antiderivative> of the squared <hyperbolic secant>:
$$
\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}f(x)\,dx
=\frac12\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}\operatorname{sech}^2t\,dt
=\frac12\big[\tanh t\big]_{-\infty}^{\infty}
=\boxed{1}.
$$
For completeness, the same expression with $s<0$ integrates to $-1$, since the transformed limits reverse. If a signed scale were desired, replacing its prefactor by $1/(4|s|)$ would give a valid density, but that is a correction to the printed formula.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For $s>0$, integrate the <probability density function> to obtain the <cumulative distribution function>
$$
F_X(x)=\frac12\left[1+\tanh\!\left(\frac{x-\mu}{2s}\right)\right].
$$
If $\mu>0$, the requested <probability> is $F_X(\mu)-F_X(0)$. Since the <hyperbolic tangent> is odd, this gives
$$
\boxed{\mathbb P(0<X<\mu)=
\begin{cases}
\dfrac12\tanh\!\left(\dfrac{\mu}{2s}\right),&\mu>0,\\
0,&\mu\leq0.
\end{cases}}
$$
The second case matters because the question places no sign restriction on $\mu$: the event is empty when its upper endpoint is nonpositive. Using an oriented <integral> from $0$ to a negative $\mu$ would give a negative number, not a <probability>.

= iii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=iii}

The same <integration by substitution> gives the <expected value>
$$
\mathbb E[X]=\frac12\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}(\mu+2st)\operatorname{sech}^2t\,dt
=\mu+s\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}t\operatorname{sech}^2t\,dt.
$$
The remaining integrand is an <odd function>. Its <integral> is absolutely convergent, since $\operatorname{sech}^2t\leq4e^{-2|t|}$ and $|t|e^{-2|t|}$ is integrable. Thus cancellation under <symmetry in integration> is legitimate, rather than merely a principal-value calculation. Consequently
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[X]=\mu.}
$$
Equivalently, the <logistic distribution> is symmetric about $\mu$, and its exponentially decaying tails ensure that its <expected value> exists.

= 17
{parent=Paper 1}
