= Solution
{parent=i}

Use <integration by substitution> with $u=\ln x$, so $du=dx/x$. For $x>1$, also $u>0$, and
$$
\int\frac{dx}{x\ln x}=\int\frac{du}{u}=\boxed{\ln(\ln x)+C}.
$$
The domain condition explains why no absolute value is needed around the inner logarithm.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

Separate one factor of the <hyperbolic sine> and use the <hyperbolic Pythagorean identity> $\sinh^2x=\cosh^2x-1$. With $u=\cosh x$ and $du=\sinh x\,dx$, <integration by substitution> gives
$$
\int\frac{\sinh^3x}{\cosh^2x}\,dx
=\int\frac{u^2-1}{u^2}\,du
=\int(1-u^{-2})\,du
=\boxed{\cosh x+\frac1{\cosh x}+C}.
$$
For real $x$, the <hyperbolic cosine> never vanishes. Differentiating the result gives $\sinh x(1-\operatorname{sech}^2x)=\sinh^3x/\cosh^2x$, checking the sign of the reciprocal term.

= b
{parent=17}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Use <integration by parts> in $I_{n+1}$, writing the integrand as $x^{2n+1}(xe^{-x^2})$. Since an <antiderivative> of $xe^{-x^2}$ is $-e^{-x^2}/2$,
$$
\begin{aligned}
I_{n+1}
&=\left[-\frac12x^{2n+1}e^{-x^2}\right]_{-\infty}^{\infty}
+\frac{2n+1}{2}\int_{-\infty}^{\infty}x^{2n}e^{-x^2}\,dx\\
&=\boxed{\left(n+\frac12\right)I_n}.
\end{aligned}
$$
The boundary term vanishes because the Gaussian <exponential function> decays faster than any fixed power. These <improper integrals> are absolutely convergent, justifying the limiting <integration by parts>.

Starting from the permitted <Gaussian integral> $I_0=\sqrt\pi$, the <Gaussian moment recurrence> gives $I_1=\sqrt\pi/2$ and then
$$
\boxed{I_2=\frac34\sqrt\pi,\qquad I_3=\frac{15}{8}\sqrt\pi.}
$$
More generally, iteration gives $I_n=(2n-1)!!\sqrt\pi/2^n$, using the <double factorial> with $(-1)!!=1$.

