= Solution
{parent=a}

A row's squared <Euclidean norm> is $[(1+\sqrt3)^2+1+(1-\sqrt3)^2]/6=3/2$, and the <dot product> of the two rows is $[2(1-3)+1]/6=-1/2$. Thus the row <Gram matrix> is
$$
\boxed{AA^{\mathsf T}=\begin{pmatrix}3/2&-1/2\\-1/2&3/2\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
For the column <Gram matrix>, the first and third squared column norms are $4/3$, the middle one is $1/3$, and the respective cross-products are $1/3,-2/3,1/3$. Hence
$$
\boxed{A^{\mathsf T}A=\frac13\begin{pmatrix}4&1&-2\\1&1&1\\-2&1&4\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
The two products are square <matrices> of different sizes; their unequal dimensions are consistent with $A$ being rectangular.

= b
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For a <matrix> with equal diagonal entries, the symmetric and antisymmetric directions $(1,1)$ and $(1,-1)$ are <eigenvectors>. Direct multiplication of $AA^{\mathsf T}$ gives <eigenvalues> $3/2-1/2=1$ and $3/2+1/2=2$. Dividing each direction by its <Euclidean norm> gives
$$
\boxed{\lambda_1=1,\quad \mathbf u_1=\frac1{\sqrt2}\begin{pmatrix}1\\1\end{pmatrix};\qquad
\lambda_2=2,\quad \mathbf u_2=\frac1{\sqrt2}\begin{pmatrix}1\\-1\end{pmatrix}.}
$$
The two normalized <eigenvectors> are <orthogonal>; either may independently be replaced by its negative.

= c
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

In $A^{\mathsf T}A$, every row sums to one, so $(1,1,1)$ is an <eigenvector> with <eigenvalue> $1$. The antisymmetric direction $(1,0,-1)$ gives <eigenvalue> $2$. A direction orthogonal to both is $(1,-2,1)$, and direct multiplication shows that it lies in the <kernel of a linear map>. Normalizing all three gives the complete system
$$
\boxed{\begin{array}{c|c}
\sigma_1=1&\mathbf v_1=\dfrac1{\sqrt3}(1,1,1)^{\mathsf T}\\[3pt]
\sigma_2=2&\mathbf v_2=\dfrac1{\sqrt2}(1,0,-1)^{\mathsf T}\\[3pt]
\sigma_3=0&\mathbf v_3=\dfrac1{\sqrt6}(1,-2,1)^{\mathsf T}
\end{array}}
$$
They form an <orthonormal basis> of $\mathbb R^3$. The extra zero <eigenvalue> reflects the one-dimensional <kernel of a linear map> for this rank-two rectangular <matrix>. The question's symbols $\sigma_m$ denote the <eigenvalues> of $A^{\mathsf T}A$; the nonzero <singular values> of $A$ are their <square roots>, namely $1$ and $\sqrt2$.

= d
{parent=18}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Using the signs chosen above, explicit multiplication gives
$$
\boxed{A\mathbf v_1=\frac1{\sqrt2}\begin{pmatrix}1\\1\end{pmatrix}=\mathbf u_1,\qquad
A\mathbf v_2=\begin{pmatrix}1\\-1\end{pmatrix}=\sqrt2\,\mathbf u_2,\qquad
A\mathbf v_3=\mathbf0.}
$$
Thus the nonzero cases obey $A\mathbf v_m=\sqrt{\sigma_m}\,\mathbf u_m$. This relates the <right singular vectors> and <left singular vectors> in a <singular value decomposition>. For example, if $U=(\mathbf u_1\ \mathbf u_2)$ and $V=(\mathbf v_1\ \mathbf v_2\ \mathbf v_3)$, then $A=U\Sigma V^{\mathsf T}$ with $\Sigma=\begin{pmatrix}1&0&0\\0&\sqrt2&0\end{pmatrix}$.

