= Solution
{parent=a}

The <Newton root-finding iteration> follows the current <tangent line> to its horizontal-axis intersection. Since $f'(x)=2x$, it gives
$$
\boxed{x_{n+1}=x_n-\frac{x_n^2-a}{2x_n}
=\frac12\left(x_n+\frac a{x_n}\right),\qquad x_n\ne0.}
$$
A positive initial guess is appropriate for the positive <square root>; negative starting values instead target the negative root.

= b
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Write $r=\sqrt a>0$ and subtract it from the iteration:
$$
e_{n+1}=\frac{x_n^2+a-2rx_n}{2x_n}
=\frac{(x_n-r)^2}{2x_n}
=\boxed{\frac{e_n^2}{2x_n}}.
$$
This is an <exact Newton error identity for a quadratic>. If $x_n>r$, then $x_n>0$ and $e_n>0$, so $e_{n+1}>0$. Induction from $x_0>r$ proves **$x_n>r$ for every $n$**. Also $x_{n+1}-x_n=(a-x_n^2)/(2x_n)<0$, so the iterates decrease while remaining above the root. Their limit must satisfy $L=(L+a/L)/2$, hence $L=r$. The squared error numerator explains the ensuing <quadratic convergence>.

= c
{parent=19}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The supplied bracket gives $0<e_0=1.5-\sqrt2<0.1$. Part (b) ensures $x_n>\sqrt2>1.4$, so each error satisfies
$$
0<e_{n+1}<\frac{e_n^2}{2.8}.
$$
Writing $d_n=e_n/2.8$ gives $d_{n+1}<d_n^2$ and $d_0<0.1/2.8=1/28$. After three steps the exponent has doubled three times:
$$
\boxed{0<e_3<\frac{2.8}{28^8}\approx7.4113\times10^{-12}<10^{-11}.}
$$
This rigorous <error bound> uses only the given bracket, not advance knowledge of the digits of $\sqrt2$.

The actual rational iterates are $x_1=17/12$, $x_2=577/408$, and $x_3=665857/470832\approx1.414213562374690$. They illustrate the bound, but the proof comes from the exact error identity and the positive lower bound on the denominators.

= d
{parent=19}
{scope}

= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

The function $g(x)=1/(x\ln x)$ is positive and decreasing for $x\geq2$, since $g'(x)=-(\ln x+1)/(x^2\ln^2x)<0$. The <integral test for convergence> therefore applies. Its comparison <improper integral> is
$$
\int_2^R\frac{dx}{x\ln x}=\ln(\ln R)-\ln(\ln2)\longrightarrow+\infty.
$$
Consequently **$\displaystyle\sum_{n=2}^{\infty}1/(n\ln n)$ diverges**.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The function $g(x)=1/(x\ln^2x)$ is likewise positive and decreasing on $[2,\infty)$, with $g'(x)=-(\ln x+2)/(x^2\ln^3x)<0$. Now
$$
\int_2^\infty\frac{dx}{x\ln^2x}
=\left[-\frac1{\ln x}\right]_2^\infty=\frac1{\ln2}<\infty.
$$
The <integral test for convergence> shows that **$\displaystyle\sum_{n=2}^{\infty}1/(n\ln^2n)$ converges**. This comparison <integral> determines convergence, not the numerical value of the series itself.

= 20
{parent=Paper 1}
