= a
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

The original <differential equation> is defined only where $x\ne0$ and $y\ne0$. Put $w=y^3$, so the <chain rule> gives $w'=3y^2y'$. Multiplying the equation by $3y^2$ yields a first-order <linear ordinary differential equation>:
$$
w'=2x^2+\frac wx,\qquad w'-\frac wx=2x^2.
$$
An <integrating factor> on either half-line is $1/x$, up to an irrelevant nonzero constant. Equivalently, apply the <quotient rule> directly:
$$
\left(\frac wx\right)'=\frac{w'-w/x}{x}=2x.
$$
Integrating gives $w/x=x^2+C$. Thus the real <general solution> is
$$
\boxed{y(x)=\sqrt[3]{x^3+Cx},\qquad C\in\mathbb R,}
$$
on any connected interval where $x\ne0$ and $x^3+Cx\ne0$. These restrictions preserve both the original denominator and differentiability of the real cube root. In particular $C=0$ includes $y=x$ on either half-line; this solution has not been lost through division by an expression involving $y/x-1$.

For verification, differentiating $y^3=x^3+Cx$ gives $3y^2y'=3x^2+C$, while $(2x^3+y^3)/x=3x^2+C$. The two sides agree wherever the original equation is defined.

= b
{parent=20}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The seven requested <parabolas> are $y=cx^2$ for $c=-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3$; the case $c=0$ is the horizontal axis. Positive $c$ opens upward, negative $c$ downward, and larger $|c|$ gives a narrower <parabola>. All pass through the origin with a horizontal <tangent line>.

To find <orthogonal trajectories>, use the continuous family $y=cx^2$ containing these seven representative members. Its slope is $y'=2cx=2y/x$ away from $x=0$. Two finite slopes of perpendicular tangents multiply to $-1$, so the orthogonal slope is
$$
\frac{dy}{dx}=-\frac{x}{2y}.
$$
This <separable differential equation> gives $2y\,dy=-x\,dx$, hence
$$
\boxed{x^2+2y^2=C,\qquad C>0.}
$$
These <ellipses> are the regular real trajectories; $C=0$ is a single point, not a regular curve, and $C<0$ has no real points. One example is $x^2+2y^2=2$, with semiaxes $\sqrt2$ horizontally and $1$ vertically.

The implicit tangent relation $2x\,dx+4y\,dy=0$ also describes points with vertical tangents, where the slope quotient is unavailable. At intersections with the member $y=0$, the <ellipse> has a vertical <tangent line> and is perpendicular to that horizontal axis. The common origin of the <parabolas> is not on any regular trajectory with $C>0$. The sketch shows the requested seven members and the example <ellipse>.

\Image[paper-1-orthogonal-trajectories.png]
{title=Seven parabolic family members and an elliptical orthogonal trajectory}
